ned</span><span class=\"text-gold-dark\">Star</span></span></span></a><div class=\"hidden items-center gap-7 md:flex\"><a class=\"text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight transition-colors text-ink/70 hover:text-ink\" href=\"/\">Manifesto</a><a class=\"text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight transition-colors text-ink/70 hover:text-ink\" href=\"/pricing\">Pricing</a><a class=\"text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight transition-colors text-ink/70 hover:text-ink\" href=\"/audit\">The Audit</a><a class=\"text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight transition-colors text-ink/70 hover:text-ink\" href=\"/yotpo-refugees\">Yotpo Refugees</a><a class=\"text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight transition-colors text-ink/70 hover:text-ink\" href=\"/reviews/expedia-parts\">Live Store</a><a class=\"text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight transition-colors text-ink/70 hover:text-ink\" href=\"/design-lab/brand\">Brand</a></div><div class=\"hidden items-center gap-3 md:flex\"><span class=\"smallcaps hidden text-[10px] lg:inline text-ink/70\">Vol. 01 · 2026</span><a class=\"rounded-full px-4 py-2 text-[13px] font-bold text-ink shadow-sm transition-shadow gold-foil hover:shadow-md\" href=\"/signup\">Start free</a></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"rounded-md p-2 md:hidden text-ink\" aria-label=\"Open menu\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-menu\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M4 5h16\"></path><path d=\"M4 12h16\"></path><path d=\"M4 19h16\"></path></svg></button></nav></header><article class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6\"><div class=\"mb-3 flex items-center gap-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-text-muted\"><span>Reviews and conversion</span><span aria-hidden=\"true\">·</span><span>5<!-- --> min read</span></div><h1 class=\"text-3xl font-bold text-navy sm:text-4xl\">How review velocity shapes conversion on a product page</h1><p class=\"mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-muted\">A product with a steady flow of new reviews reads differently than one with a stale pile from a year ago. Here&#x27;s why velocity, not just count, moves conversion.</p><div class=\"mt-8 space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink\"><h1 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>How review velocity shapes conversion on a product page</span></h1><p class=\"text-ink\"><strong>Quick answer.</strong><span> Review velocity — how often new reviews land, not the total count — shapes conversion because shoppers read recency as relevance. A steady trickle of fresh reviews reads as actively bought and true right now; a large pile that stopped growing months ago reads as a snapshot of the past, even at the same average rating.</span></p><h2 class=\"pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>What is review velocity, and how is it different from review count?</span></h2><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Count is a total. Velocity is a rate — how frequently new reviews arrive relative to how long the product has been listed.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Two products can carry the identical count and read completely differently. A product with 150 reviews, the last one posted eleven months ago, tells a shopper the review activity has gone quiet. A product with 150 reviews, ten of them from the last month, tells a shopper the product is still being bought and still holding up under current scrutiny. Same number. Different signal.</span></p><h2 class=\"pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>Why does recency change how a shopper reads the reviews?</span></h2><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Because a shopper isn&#x27;t asking &quot;did this used to work.&quot; They&#x27;re asking &quot;does this work now.&quot;</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Products change — a supplier switches, a manufacturing run shifts, packaging gets redesigned, a formula gets tweaked. None of that necessarily shows up anywhere on the page except in how recent the reviews are. A large batch of old reviews describes a version of the product a shopper can&#x27;t be sure they&#x27;re about to receive. A handful of current reviews describes what&#x27;s shipping this week.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>That&#x27;s also why review count alone is a weaker predictor than it looks. A product can accumulate reviews for years and still stall on velocity the moment attention moves elsewhere — new SKUs, a redesign, a marketing push somewhere else — and conversion tends to follow that stall down even while the total count keeps climbing on paper.</span></p><h2 class=\"pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>Can more reviews, arriving faster, ever work against a product?</span></h2><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Yes — if what&#x27;s arriving is bad news.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Velocity cuts both ways. A rising rate of fresh, specific complaints is a faster and more visible signal to a shopper than the same complaints diluted into a large historical average. A product with a sudden run of one- and two-star reviews in the last two weeks, even sitting on a decent lifetime average, is telling a shopper something urgent is happening right now — a defect, a supply issue, a change nobody announced.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>This is actually the useful side of tracking velocity: it surfaces a real problem faster than a slow-moving average ever would, giving a merchant a chance to catch and fix it before it becomes the defining story of the product.</span></p><h2 class=\"pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>What actually drives review velocity, in practice?</span></h2><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>Not a single campaign — a consistent process running behind every order.</span></p><div class=\"overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border\"><table class=\"w-full border-collapse text-sm\"><thead><tr class=\"bg-surface\"><th class=\"border-b border-border px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-navy\"><span>Approach</span></th><th class=\"border-b border-border px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-navy\"><span>Effect on velocity</span></th><th class=\"border-b border-border px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-navy\"><span>Trade-off</span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"border-b border-border last:border-b-0\"><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>One bulk request campaign, run occasionally</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>A spike, then silence</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>Looks stale again within weeks</span></td></tr><tr class=\"border-b border-border last:border-b-0\"><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>A request sent at a consistent point after every delivery</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>A steady, ongoing trickle</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>Requires the request to actually run on every order</span></td></tr><tr class=\"border-b border-border last:border-b-0\"><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>Requests sent only to customers expected to respond well</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>A flattering but thin, irregular flow</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>Produces a biased sample, not real velocity</span></td></tr><tr class=\"border-b border-border last:border-b-0\"><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>No systematic request process</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>Velocity depends entirely on unprompted reviewers</span></td><td class=\"px-3 py-2 align-top text-text-muted\"><span>Almost always the slowest and least representative option</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>The pattern underneath: velocity isn&#x27;t something to chase with a burst of effort. It&#x27;s a byproduct of asking every customer, at a predictable moment, every time an order closes.</span></p><h2 class=\"pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>What should a merchant actually watch, beyond the average rating?</span></h2><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>The trend line, not just the number.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><span>A rating average is a lagging summary. Velocity — how many reviews land per week or month, and which direction the sentiment inside them is moving — is a leading indicator. Watching it catches a supply problem or a shipping delay while it&#x27;s still small, instead of finding out three months later that the average quietly slid while nobody was checking the pace of what was coming in.</span></p><h2 class=\"pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-navy\"><span>Frequently asked questions</span></h2><p class=\"text-ink\"><strong>What does &quot;review velocity&quot; mean?</strong><span> The rate at which new reviews land on a product over time, as opposed to the total count sitting on the page. A product with 200 reviews and none in the last eight months has a different velocity than one adding a handful every week, even at the same total.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><strong>Why does a steady flow of new reviews matter more than a big one-time batch?</strong><span> Because shoppers read recency as a proxy for whether the product is still what the reviews describe. A pile of reviews from two years ago says less about the product being sold today than five reviews from the last month do.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><strong>Can review velocity ever hurt conversion?</strong><span> Yes, if the recent reviews are trending negative. A rising rate of fresh complaints is a faster, clearer signal to a shopper than the same complaints buried in an old average — which is exactly why velocity should be watched, not just accumulated.</span></p><p class=\"text-ink\"><strong>How does a merchant actually influence review velocity?</strong><span> By requesting a review from every completed order at a consistent point after delivery, rather than running occasional bulk campaigns. Steady requests produce a steady trickle of new reviews; sporadic campaigns produce spikes followed by long silences.</span></p></div><section class=\"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-6\"><h2 class=\"mb-3 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-text-muted\">Sources</h2><ul class=\"space-y-2 text-sm\"><li><a href=\"https://wiremo.co/business/ratings-and-reviews-affect-conversion-rates/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"break-all text-navy-light hover:underline\">https://wiremo.co/business/ratings-and-reviews-affect-conversion-rates/</a></li></ul></section><p class=\"mt-12\"><a class=\"text-sm text-text-muted hover:text-navy hover:underline\" href=\"/blog\">← All articles</a></p><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"What does \\\"review velocity\\\" mean?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"The rate at which new reviews land on a product over time, as opposed to the total count sitting on the page. A product with 200 reviews and none in the last eight months has a different velocity than one adding a handful every week, even at the same total.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Why does a steady flow of new reviews matter more than a big one-time batch?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Because shoppers read recency as a proxy for whether the product is still what the reviews describe. A pile of reviews from two years ago says less about the product being sold today than five reviews from the last month do.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"Can review velocity ever hurt conversion?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Yes, if the recent reviews are trending negative. A rising rate of fresh complaints is a faster, clearer signal to a shopper than the same complaints buried in an old average — which is exactly why velocity should be watched, not just accumulated.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How does a merchant actually influence review velocity?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"By requesting a review from every completed order at a consistent point after delivery, rather than running occasional bulk campaigns. Steady requests produce a steady trickle of new reviews; sporadic campaigns produce spikes followed by long silences.\"}}]}</script></article><footer class=\"border-t border-border bg-navy text-white\" data-scroll-theme=\"dark\"><section class=\"py-20 text-center\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4\"><h2 class=\"font-display text-3xl italic text-gold\">Stop guessing if customers trust you.</h2><p class=\"mt-4 text-white/70\">Show them proof. 1,200+ stores collect verified reviews with EarnedStar.</p><a class=\"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg border font-semibold transition duration-200 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-navy bg-gold text-ink hover:bg-gold-light border-transparent shadow-[var(--shadow-gold)] h-12 px-6 text-base mt-8\" href=\"/signup\">Start Your Free Trial →</a></div></section><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-7xl border-t border-white/10 px-4 py-12\"><div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-10 md:grid-cols-[2fr_1fr_1fr_1fr_1fr]\"><div><span class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-2\"><svg width=\"37\" height=\"37\" viewBox=\"0 0 37 37\" overflow=\"visible\" class=\"shrink-0\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M15.63,9.32 Q18.50,2.15 21.37,9.32 L21.62,9.95 Q22.72,12.69 25.67,12.89 L26.35,12.93 Q34.05,13.45 28.12,18.39 L27.60,18.83 Q25.33,20.72 26.05,23.58 L26.22,24.24 Q28.11,31.73 21.58,27.61 L21.00,27.25 Q18.50,25.68 16.00,27.25 L15.42,27.61 Q8.89,31.73 10.78,24.24 L10.95,23.58 Q11.67,20.72 9.40,18.83 L8.88,18.39 Q2.95,13.45 10.65,12.93 L11.33,12.89 Q14.28,12.69 15.38,9.95 Z\" fill=